1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:04,408
At the Corner Cafe, Asha's oldest function
closes the day.

2
00:00:04,428 --> 00:00:09,450
It adds the tax, prints a line, and
changes a week total, all at once.

3
00:00:09,500 --> 00:00:13,065
When one part goes wrong, she has to read
all of it.

4
00:00:13,085 --> 00:00:18,717
And Chapter 16 ended with a question: day
total has a variable called total inside

5
00:00:18,737 --> 00:00:18,924
it.

6
00:00:18,944 --> 00:00:21,337
Can the rest of the program see it?

7
00:00:21,953 --> 00:00:27,669
This is Code Quest, Chapter 17: where
variables live, and how to design small

8
00:00:27,689 --> 00:00:28,413
functions.

9
00:00:28,433 --> 00:00:34,315
And an AI assistant will split Asha's
function, and promise the output stays the

10
00:00:34,335 --> 00:00:34,684
same.

11
00:00:34,704 --> 00:00:36,823
Her saved output will decide.

12
00:00:37,420 --> 00:00:42,660
Here is today's mission: Asha's day report
from Chapter 16, redesigned.

13
00:00:42,680 --> 00:00:46,290
Three functions, and each one returns its
result.

14
00:00:46,333 --> 00:00:48,678
Only the last three lines print.

15
00:00:48,698 --> 00:00:49,786
Let's build it.

16
00:00:50,383 --> 00:00:55,653
Your goals: know where a variable lives,
fix two scope errors, use default and

17
00:00:55,673 --> 00:01:00,942
keyword arguments, and split a function
that does too much, with an AI's help.

18
00:01:01,517 --> 00:01:04,370
Real programs have hundreds of functions.

19
00:01:04,390 --> 00:01:10,046
A small function that gets its values as
parameters and returns its result can be

20
00:01:10,066 --> 00:01:13,550
tested on its own, and an AI can change it
safely.

21
00:01:14,150 --> 00:01:19,495
The scope of a variable is the part of the
program where its name can be used.

22
00:01:19,515 --> 00:01:22,659
A local variable is created inside a
function.

23
00:01:22,679 --> 00:01:25,410
It exists only while that function runs.

24
00:01:25,433 --> 00:01:29,143
A global variable is created at the top
level of the file.

25
00:01:29,163 --> 00:01:30,879
Every function can read it.

26
00:01:31,487 --> 00:01:32,648
Think of the cafe.

27
00:01:32,668 --> 00:01:37,502
The menu board on the wall is global:
everyone can read it, even the cook.

28
00:01:37,522 --> 00:01:42,357
The kitchen whiteboard is local: its notes
are wiped when the job is done.

29
00:01:42,400 --> 00:01:46,952
Where it stops working: suppose the cook
writes a note with the same name as

30
00:01:46,972 --> 00:01:48,637
something on the menu board.

31
00:01:48,657 --> 00:01:53,630
Python treats that name as a kitchen note
for the whole function, even on the lines

32
00:01:53,650 --> 00:01:54,593
before the note.

33
00:01:55,187 --> 00:02:00,667
So good functions follow one rule: pass
values in as parameters, and return

34
00:02:00,687 --> 00:02:01,547
results out.

35
00:02:01,567 --> 00:02:07,321
A function that uses only its parameters
and returns its result, without printing

36
00:02:07,341 --> 00:02:11,313
or changing anything outside, is called a
pure function.

37
00:02:11,333 --> 00:02:12,810
One line can test it.

38
00:02:13,420 --> 00:02:14,235
Quick check.

39
00:02:14,255 --> 00:02:17,016
A variable is created inside a function.

40
00:02:17,036 --> 00:02:18,477
Where can you use it?

41
00:02:29,847 --> 00:02:29,981
C.

42
00:02:30,001 --> 00:02:33,145
Only inside that function, while it runs.

43
00:02:33,165 --> 00:02:34,072
It is local.

44
00:02:34,687 --> 00:02:35,844
Take a breather.

45
00:02:35,864 --> 00:02:39,083
Next, Asha tests these rules with real
code.

46
00:02:43,817 --> 00:02:44,387
Step 1.

47
00:02:44,407 --> 00:02:49,191
Inside day total, line 3 creates a
variable called total.

48
00:02:49,211 --> 00:02:50,877
That makes it local.

49
00:02:50,900 --> 00:02:54,710
Line 8 calls the function and prints what
it returns.

50
00:02:54,730 --> 00:02:57,240
Line 9 tries to print total itself.

51
00:03:08,613 --> 00:03:12,253
Line 8 prints 7.5, the returned value.

52
00:03:12,300 --> 00:03:14,578
Line 9 stops with a Name Error.

53
00:03:14,598 --> 00:03:17,691
Total existed only while the function ran.

54
00:03:17,711 --> 00:03:21,249
To use a result outside, return it and
store it.

55
00:03:21,853 --> 00:03:22,389
Step 2.

56
00:03:22,409 --> 00:03:26,991
Line 2 creates tax rate at the top level,
so it is global.

57
00:03:27,011 --> 00:03:29,213
With tax reads it on line 5.

58
00:03:29,233 --> 00:03:31,263
Line 7 calls with tax.

59
00:03:31,283 --> 00:03:34,152
Line 8 changes tax rate to 1.2.

60
00:03:34,172 --> 00:03:38,345
And line 9 makes exactly the same call
again.

61
00:03:49,713 --> 00:03:50,773
3.85, then 4.2.

62
00:03:50,793 --> 00:03:56,677
The same call gives two results, because
the function reads tax rate at the moment

63
00:03:56,697 --> 00:03:57,253
it runs.

64
00:03:57,300 --> 00:04:01,409
Nothing in the call shows that hidden link
to line 8.

65
00:04:01,429 --> 00:04:06,162
That is why good functions take what they
need as parameters.

66
00:04:06,750 --> 00:04:07,326
Step 3.

67
00:04:07,346 --> 00:04:12,860
Asha counts sales with a global variable,
sales count, on line 2.

68
00:04:12,900 --> 00:04:16,329
Line 5 assigns to sales count inside the
function.

69
00:04:16,349 --> 00:04:21,641
Remember the rule: an assignment makes the
name local for the whole function.

70
00:04:33,013 --> 00:04:33,730
On line 5.

71
00:04:33,750 --> 00:04:38,521
The right side reads the local sales count
before it has a value.

72
00:04:38,541 --> 00:04:41,543
Python calls this an Unbound Local Error.

73
00:04:41,567 --> 00:04:46,557
The traceback shows the call on line 7,
then the failing line 5 inside the

74
00:04:46,577 --> 00:04:47,167
function.

75
00:04:47,200 --> 00:04:50,752
Python has a keyword, global, that allows
the change.

76
00:04:50,772 --> 00:04:53,920
But it brings back the hidden link from
step 2.

77
00:04:53,940 --> 00:04:55,403
There is a better fix.

78
00:04:56,020 --> 00:05:01,130
Add sale now takes the count as a
parameter, and returns the count plus 1.

79
00:05:01,167 --> 00:05:04,771
Lines 6 and 7 store each returned value.

80
00:05:04,791 --> 00:05:07,490
Two sales, so line 8 prints 2.

81
00:05:08,083 --> 00:05:08,610
Step 4.

82
00:05:08,630 --> 00:05:14,473
A default argument is a value a parameter
gets when the call leaves it out.

83
00:05:14,493 --> 00:05:17,443
Here, rate equals 1.1 in the def line.

84
00:05:17,467 --> 00:05:22,976
Parameters with a default must come after
the parameters without one.

85
00:05:22,996 --> 00:05:25,219
Line 6 passes a rate of 1.2.

86
00:05:25,239 --> 00:05:27,864
Lines 5 and 7 leave the rate out.

87
00:05:39,247 --> 00:05:40,159
3.85 and 4.4.

88
00:05:40,179 --> 00:05:43,317
Both calls leave out the rate, so it is
1.1.

89
00:05:43,367 --> 00:05:46,157
Line 6 prints 4.2 with its own rate.

90
00:05:46,177 --> 00:05:49,827
Now nothing is hidden: the rate is a
parameter.

91
00:05:49,867 --> 00:05:55,607
One more fact about defaults: Python
creates a default value once, when the def

92
00:05:55,627 --> 00:05:57,649
line runs, not at each call.

93
00:05:57,669 --> 00:06:00,493
For a number, that makes no difference.

94
00:06:00,513 --> 00:06:02,097
Remember it for later.

95
00:06:02,687 --> 00:06:03,232
Step 5.

96
00:06:03,252 --> 00:06:07,347
A keyword argument names the parameter in
the call.

97
00:06:07,367 --> 00:06:11,867
On line 7, q t y equals 2 says at once
what the 2 means.

98
00:06:11,900 --> 00:06:16,321
A positional argument is matched by its
position: first to first.

99
00:06:16,341 --> 00:06:21,171
Keyword arguments can come in any order,
but after any positional ones.

100
00:06:21,191 --> 00:06:23,494
Line 8 names both, in a new order.

101
00:06:34,847 --> 00:06:36,303
1 x Scone, 2.75.

102
00:06:36,323 --> 00:06:43,317
Q t y keeps its default, 1, and the names
put each value in the right place.

103
00:06:43,367 --> 00:06:49,701
Line 6 uses the default too, and line 7
gives 2 cakes for 8.00.

104
00:06:50,320 --> 00:06:51,015
Quick check.

105
00:06:51,035 --> 00:06:53,159
Which of these calls is not allowed?

106
00:07:04,513 --> 00:07:04,632
A.

107
00:07:04,652 --> 00:07:08,717
A positional argument cannot come after a
keyword argument.

108
00:07:09,320 --> 00:07:09,969
Breather.

109
00:07:09,989 --> 00:07:11,380
Relax for a moment.

110
00:07:11,400 --> 00:07:15,764
Next, the most important design rule: one
job per function.

111
00:07:20,517 --> 00:07:21,128
Step 6.

112
00:07:21,148 --> 00:07:25,547
Day total has one job: it calculates and
returns.

113
00:07:25,567 --> 00:07:28,865
Total line has one job: it builds the
text.

114
00:07:28,885 --> 00:07:30,717
Neither function prints.

115
00:07:30,767 --> 00:07:32,567
Only the main code prints.

116
00:07:32,587 --> 00:07:36,348
And line 13 tests day total with one
comparison: True.

117
00:07:36,368 --> 00:07:40,550
A function that only prints could never be
tested like that.

118
00:07:41,150 --> 00:07:42,332
Back to the mission.

119
00:07:42,352 --> 00:07:46,720
With tax takes the rate as a default
argument, instead of a fixed number.

120
00:07:46,767 --> 00:07:53,337
Day total and report line take the rate
too, and pass it on to the next function.

121
00:07:53,367 --> 00:07:57,152
Report line has its own local variable
called total, on line 15.

122
00:07:57,172 --> 00:07:58,460
Day total has one too.

123
00:07:58,480 --> 00:08:02,027
The two never meet: each lives only inside
its own function.

124
00:08:02,067 --> 00:08:07,723
Line 19 reports Friday, a day with a
higher tax, using a keyword argument for

125
00:08:07,743 --> 00:08:08,387
the rate.

126
00:08:19,747 --> 00:08:21,663
Friday, 2 sales, 9.30.

127
00:08:21,683 --> 00:08:26,767
The rate of 1.2 travels from the call into
every function.

128
00:08:26,800 --> 00:08:31,867
Line 18 uses the default rate, and line 20
tests a total in one line: True.

129
00:08:31,887 --> 00:08:33,020
Mission complete!

130
00:08:33,067 --> 00:08:37,584
Now Asha wants to split her oldest
function, close day.

131
00:08:37,604 --> 00:08:42,039
She asks an AI assistant to help, one
stage at a time.

132
00:08:42,617 --> 00:08:48,790
This chapter's AI skill: ask an AI which
functions do too much, and how to split

133
00:08:48,810 --> 00:08:49,177
them.

134
00:08:49,200 --> 00:08:52,437
A split must not change what the program
prints.

135
00:08:52,457 --> 00:08:56,509
So save the output first, and compare it
after every change.

136
00:08:57,120 --> 00:08:58,209
Stage 1: plan.

137
00:08:58,229 --> 00:09:04,706
Asha pastes her old close day function and
asks the AI to list each job, with line

138
00:09:04,726 --> 00:09:05,339
numbers.

139
00:09:05,359 --> 00:09:06,290
No code yet.

140
00:09:06,333 --> 00:09:12,403
The AI finds three jobs: the tax and the
total, a printed line, and the global week

141
00:09:12,423 --> 00:09:12,843
total.

142
00:09:12,863 --> 00:09:15,704
Asha ticks every line against the list.

143
00:09:16,320 --> 00:09:17,466
Stage 2: write.

144
00:09:17,486 --> 00:09:23,996
Before asking for a split, Asha writes the
calculation herself, as a pure day total.

145
00:09:24,016 --> 00:09:27,960
The AI predicts 11.28 and 8.53, and her
run agrees.

146
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:33,253
Then she runs the original program and
saves its real output.

147
00:09:33,273 --> 00:09:36,365
That is her target for every change.

148
00:09:36,987 --> 00:09:38,371
Stage 3: generate.

149
00:09:38,391 --> 00:09:45,002
Asha asks for a split with clear rules: no
global variables, return instead of print,

150
00:09:45,022 --> 00:09:47,187
and exactly the same output.

151
00:09:47,233 --> 00:09:52,165
The AI writes day total and report line,
and promises the same output.

152
00:09:52,185 --> 00:09:55,631
Its day total matches the one Asha wrote
herself.

153
00:09:56,253 --> 00:09:58,178
Here is the whole split.

154
00:09:58,198 --> 00:10:01,095
Look at line 10, inside report line.

155
00:10:01,115 --> 00:10:03,445
Then look at lines 14 and 15.

156
00:10:14,813 --> 00:10:15,017
No.

157
00:10:15,037 --> 00:10:16,877
Two extra lines say None.

158
00:10:16,897 --> 00:10:21,343
Report line prints instead of returning,
so it returns None.

159
00:10:21,367 --> 00:10:24,132
Lines 14 and 15 print that None too.

160
00:10:24,152 --> 00:10:27,072
Asha's saved target caught it at once.

161
00:10:27,092 --> 00:10:30,708
The AI broke its own rule, and it sounded
sure.

162
00:10:31,320 --> 00:10:32,328
Stage 4: test.

163
00:10:32,348 --> 00:10:34,678
Asha tells the AI what happened.

164
00:10:34,698 --> 00:10:40,406
She asks for the fix, and for three test
cases with the exact expected line: a

165
00:10:40,426 --> 00:10:43,270
normal day, an empty day, and one sale.

166
00:10:43,300 --> 00:10:48,032
The AI changes print to return on line 10,
and lists the three cases.

167
00:10:48,052 --> 00:10:53,403
Asha checks Monday against her target, and
counts the spaces in the other two.

168
00:10:54,020 --> 00:10:58,098
First, she runs the three cases on the
AI's first version.

169
00:10:58,118 --> 00:11:02,902
Every case fails, because report line
still prints and returns None.

170
00:11:03,520 --> 00:11:09,510
Lines 12 to 14 hold the three cases: a
day, its prices, and the expected line.

171
00:11:09,530 --> 00:11:13,670
The loop on line 15 runs each one through
report line.

172
00:11:13,700 --> 00:11:18,442
Line 17 prints pass or FAIL, with the if
else from Chapter 14.

173
00:11:18,462 --> 00:11:21,515
All three pass, including the empty day.

174
00:11:22,120 --> 00:11:23,194
Stage 5: debug.

175
00:11:23,214 --> 00:11:28,010
Asha moves the week total into its own
function, and Python stops.

176
00:11:28,030 --> 00:11:33,044
She pastes the code and the full error,
and asks for the cause first.

177
00:11:33,653 --> 00:11:39,512
The AI explains the cause well: line 5
assigns to week total, so it is local, and

178
00:11:39,532 --> 00:11:41,908
it is read before it has a value.

179
00:11:41,928 --> 00:11:44,883
But its fix is to add the global keyword.

180
00:11:44,933 --> 00:11:48,395
That breaks Asha's own plan: no global
variables.

181
00:11:48,415 --> 00:11:53,866
So she asks for a fix that passes the week
total in, and returns the new one.

182
00:11:54,487 --> 00:11:59,604
Add to week now takes the week total in,
and returns the new one.

183
00:11:59,624 --> 00:12:02,687
Lines 19 and 20 add Monday and Tuesday.

184
00:12:02,733 --> 00:12:04,968
Asha runs everything again.

185
00:12:04,988 --> 00:12:11,313
The output matches her target exactly,
week total 19.81, and all three cases

186
00:12:11,333 --> 00:12:11,730
pass.

187
00:12:12,353 --> 00:12:13,658
Stage 6: improve.

188
00:12:13,678 --> 00:12:18,413
Asha asks for one improvement that keeps
the output the same.

189
00:12:18,433 --> 00:12:24,113
The AI suggests a record sale function
with a default empty list, so she can

190
00:12:24,133 --> 00:12:25,913
record sales one by one.

191
00:12:26,520 --> 00:12:29,394
Asha tests it with two separate days.

192
00:12:29,414 --> 00:12:32,366
Lines 6 and 7 record two Monday sales.

193
00:12:32,386 --> 00:12:36,825
Line 8 records one Tuesday sale, and
leaves out the list.

194
00:12:48,180 --> 00:12:48,374
No.

195
00:12:48,394 --> 00:12:50,653
Both lists hold all three sales.

196
00:12:50,673 --> 00:12:56,210
Python creates a default value once, when
the def line runs, not at each call.

197
00:12:56,233 --> 00:13:01,583
Remember step 4: the default is created
once, so every call that leaves out the

198
00:13:01,603 --> 00:13:02,603
list shares it.

199
00:13:02,623 --> 00:13:07,768
Asha rejects the suggestion, and her final
program still matches the target.

200
00:13:08,387 --> 00:13:09,201
Quick check.

201
00:13:09,221 --> 00:13:13,303
An AI split your function, and says the
output is the same.

202
00:13:13,323 --> 00:13:14,346
What do you do?

203
00:13:25,713 --> 00:13:25,834
D.

204
00:13:25,854 --> 00:13:29,073
Run it, and compare with the output you
saved.

205
00:13:29,687 --> 00:13:30,314
Breather.

206
00:13:30,334 --> 00:13:35,707
Asha caught three AI problems: a print
instead of a return, a fix that used

207
00:13:35,727 --> 00:13:38,008
global, and a list as a default.

208
00:13:42,750 --> 00:13:43,696
Now you try.

209
00:13:43,716 --> 00:13:47,160
Line 3 creates message inside the
function.

210
00:13:47,180 --> 00:13:48,529
Line 6 prints it.

211
00:13:59,880 --> 00:14:00,759
A Name Error.

212
00:14:00,779 --> 00:14:04,910
Message is local, so it disappeared when
the function ended.

213
00:14:04,933 --> 00:14:08,820
The fix: return message, and store the
returned value.

214
00:14:09,420 --> 00:14:12,370
Mistake 2 is the quick check from step 5.

215
00:14:12,390 --> 00:14:18,237
After a keyword argument, every argument
needs a name, so Python cannot even read

216
00:14:18,257 --> 00:14:18,889
the file.

217
00:14:19,487 --> 00:14:20,072
One more.

218
00:14:20,092 --> 00:14:23,840
Look at the def line, and remember the
rule from step 4.

219
00:14:35,213 --> 00:14:40,339
The parameter with a default, rate, comes
before price, which has none.

220
00:14:40,359 --> 00:14:42,803
Parameters with a default go last.

221
00:14:42,833 --> 00:14:46,029
The fix: price first, then rate equals
1.1.

222
00:14:46,620 --> 00:14:50,731
Mistake 4 is the AI's suggestion again,
with no error.

223
00:14:50,751 --> 00:14:53,026
Lina and Omar share one order.

224
00:14:53,046 --> 00:14:57,693
The fix: no default list; pass a new empty
list in each call.

225
00:14:58,287 --> 00:14:58,832
Breather.

226
00:14:58,852 --> 00:15:01,283
Two questions to think about come next.

227
00:15:06,017 --> 00:15:06,395
First.

228
00:15:06,415 --> 00:15:09,845
Report line returns its text instead of
printing it.

229
00:15:09,865 --> 00:15:14,556
How does that help if Asha later wants to
send the line somewhere else?

230
00:15:25,913 --> 00:15:28,684
Think about who decides where the text
goes.

231
00:15:29,287 --> 00:15:29,714
Second.

232
00:15:29,734 --> 00:15:33,869
A thirty line function reads input,
calculates, prints and saves.

233
00:15:33,889 --> 00:15:38,536
Which smaller functions would you split it
into, and which could be pure?

234
00:15:49,913 --> 00:15:53,944
Write the list of jobs first, as Asha did
in stage 1.

235
00:15:54,517 --> 00:15:55,315
Let's recap.

236
00:15:55,335 --> 00:15:58,245
A local variable lives inside its
function.

237
00:15:58,265 --> 00:16:03,357
A function can read a global, but its
result then depends on a hidden line.

238
00:16:03,400 --> 00:16:08,365
Assigning to a name inside a function
makes it local for the whole function.

239
00:16:08,385 --> 00:16:11,120
So pass values in, and return results out.

240
00:16:11,167 --> 00:16:16,301
Defaults make a parameter optional,
keywords name it, and a list is never a

241
00:16:16,321 --> 00:16:16,851
default.

242
00:16:16,871 --> 00:16:21,043
And with AI: save the output, and compare
after every change.

243
00:16:21,653 --> 00:16:22,966
One last check.

244
00:16:22,986 --> 00:16:27,408
With tax is called with 10.0, and rate
equals 1.2.

245
00:16:27,428 --> 00:16:29,185
What does it return?

246
00:16:40,547 --> 00:16:40,713
B.

247
00:16:40,733 --> 00:16:41,180
12.0.

248
00:16:41,200 --> 00:16:45,569
The keyword argument replaces the default
rate.

249
